"""Build self-contained English issues from actual synthetic test records."""
import json,collections
from pathlib import Path
ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent
D=json.loads((ROOT/'validation.json').read_text())
SPECS={
'groups':dict(title='GROUPS window frames for peer-aware time-series analysis',why='Trades, device readings and batch events often share a timestamp. Analysts need the previous K distinct timestamps or price levels, including every peer, rather than K physical rows or a fixed elapsed-time distance. ROWS can split peers; RANGE cannot express a count of peer groups.',contract='Implement GROUPS frame bounds with correct peer equality, partition boundaries and inclusive endpoints. In this test ORDER BY tick deliberately omits id so equal ticks form peers.',main='groups',ref='https://clickhouse.com/docs/reference/functions/window-functions'),
'exclude':dict(title='EXCLUDE frame clauses for leave-one-out and peer-excluded analytics',why='An anomaly baseline should sometimes exclude the observation being evaluated. Peer-excluded benchmarks should exclude concurrent observations at the same timestamp so a burst does not contaminate its own reference. Subtracting the current value from SUM does not generally emulate AVG, variance, non-additive aggregates or NULL frames.',contract='Support EXCLUDE CURRENT ROW, GROUP, TIES and NO OTHERS independently of frame membership. Test explicit RANGE peer frames, all-NULL/empty results, duplicate ordering keys and subsequent query usability. This is a SQL extension request: the tested ClickHouse also rejects EXCLUDE, so it is not a claimed ClickHouse-only gap.',main='exclude_current_row',ref='https://duckdb.org/docs/stable/sql/functions/window_functions.html'),
'ignore_nulls':dict(title='IGNORE NULLS and non-NULL value navigation in windows',why='Telemetry gaps, sparse prices and intermittent customer events require the previous/next observed value and the first/last valid observation in a frame. Filtering NULL rows upstream changes row counts, timestamps and alignment with other metrics, so it is not a general replacement.',contract='Support IGNORE NULLS for FIRST_VALUE, LAST_VALUE, NTH_VALUE, LAG and LEAD while preserving the original rows. Document a mapping for DolphinDB firstNot/lastNot. RESPECT NULLS is already accepted on this tested revision; this request does not reopen the completed #28031. The explicit NULL-default execution bug is separately tracked by #29925.',main='last_value_ignore_nulls',ref='https://clickhouse.com/docs/reference/functions/window-functions/last_value'),
'distinct':dict(title='DISTINCT window aggregates for exact rolling unique metrics',why='Rolling active users, distinct devices, unique fraud counterparties and deduplicated observations require exact per-frame membership. GROUP BY loses the original row stream; cumulative distinct counts cannot handle members leaving a bounded frame. Approximate counts are not a replacement when reconciliation or audit requires exactness.',contract='Support COUNT/SUM/AVG(DISTINCT ...) OVER with ROWS and RANGE. Ignore NULL according to the aggregate contract and reference-count duplicates when a frame moves. This differs from #9049, which optimizes existing grouped DISTINCT aggregation rather than implementing currently rejected window DISTINCT.',main='count_distinct',ref='https://duckdb.org/docs/stable/sql/functions/window_functions.html'),
'filter':dict(title='FILTER clauses on aggregates and window aggregates',why='A dashboard often computes successful orders, failures and valid samples over the same time frame. FILTER expresses eligibility per metric without deleting rows needed by other metrics and improves portability of PostgreSQL/DuckDB-generated analytical SQL.',contract='Accept aggregate FILTER(WHERE predicate), including its composition with OVER. COUNT(*) and SUM have different NULL/empty-frame behavior, which must be retained. SUM(CASE WHEN ... THEN value ELSE NULL END) is already a working alternative on the test fixture; this is a syntax/composability request, not a claim the math is impossible.',main='SUM(v)_filter',ref='https://duckdb.org/docs/stable/sql/query_syntax/filter.html'),
'frame_navigation':dict(title='frame-respecting lagInFrame and leadInFrame navigation',why='A feature or comparison constrained to a chosen lookback frame must not read outside that frame. Standard LAG/LEAD operate on the partition and can return a value beyond the explicit frame; this is different from ClickHouse frame-aware navigation.',contract='Add frame-aware lag/lead entry points or an explicit equivalent mode. The test frame contains only the current row, so an offset of one must return the supplied -99 default for every row. Use a typed non-NULL default to keep this request independent of #29925.',main='lagInFrame',ref='https://clickhouse.com/docs/reference/functions/window-functions/lagInFrame'),
'derivative':dict(title='non-negative time derivatives for irregular cumulative counters',why='Network-byte, energy-meter and transaction counters need a rate per unit time. Adjacent observations may be two seconds or minutes apart, so a raw value delta is not a rate. Resets require an explicit policy; the requested profile clamps a negative delta to zero.',contract='Provide nonNegativeDerivative or a documented native equivalent using ordered event time and an optional time unit. Define first-row, NULL, duplicate/non-increasing timestamps and reset behavior. The verified profile uses seconds and non-NULL counters, returning 0,3,0 for (10@0s,16@2s,5@5s). This is not the full Prometheus counter-reset extrapolation contract.',main='nonNegativeDerivative',ref='https://clickhouse.com/docs/reference/functions/window-functions/nonNegativeDerivative'),
'weighted':dict(title='native weighted sum and average aggregates with window support',why='VWAP, exposure-weighted risk, weighted model losses and sample-quality-weighted telemetry require weights, not an unweighted AVG. Centralizing paired-NULL and zero-denominator policies removes repeated error-prone dialect rewrites in pandas/Spark SQL pipelines.',contract='Implement weighted sum and weighted average (e.g. wsum/wavg or canonical SQL names) in grouped and window contexts. Define which pairs are eligible, weight types, zero total weight, negative-weight policy and accumulator precision. The tested profile has positive weights and excludes a weight when its value is NULL. Existing SUM expressions compute this profile correctly; no native-performance claim is made.',main='wavg',ref='https://docs.dolphindb.com/en/Programming/SQLStatements/analytic_functions.html'),
'pair_stats':dict(title='native correlation and covariance aggregates with window support',why='Rolling sensor correlation, asset co-movement and multi-signal drift detection need paired statistics. Independent NULL filtering for x and y is wrong: every moment must use the same eligible pairs. Hand-written subtraction of large raw moments can also be numerically unstable.',contract='Add CORR and population/sample covariance with documented aliases such as covar/COVAR_SAMP/COVAR_POP and bounded-window support. Test paired NULLs, minimum observations and zero variance. This fixture uses sample covariance and Pearson correlation. Linear-regression slope/intercept are already requested by #10182; fresh slope/REGR cases are being added there rather than filing another regression issue.',main='corr',ref='https://docs.dolphindb.com/en/Programming/SQLStatements/analytic_functions.html'),
'moments':dict(title='sum2, skewness and kurtosis aggregate/window primitives',why='Variance diagnostics, distribution-shift detection and tail-risk monitoring need squared sums and higher standardized moments. Average and standard deviation alone do not identify asymmetric or heavy-tailed changes.',contract='Provide sum of squares and explicit skewness/kurtosis conventions in grouped and window contexts. The verified profile uses sum2=SUM(v*v), biased skewness and Pearson kurtosis (normal baseline 3), with NULL for undefined zero-variance/insufficient profiles. Do not silently substitute excess kurtosis (baseline 0) or an unbiased estimator. Document ddof/bias modes separately. The squared-sum SQL expression already works.',main='skew',ref='https://docs.dolphindb.com/en/Programming/SQLStatements/analytic_functions.html'),
'product':dict(title='product aggregates with window support and zero/sign/NULL semantics',why='Compounded growth factors, survival probabilities and multiplicative scores require products over a bounded or cumulative window. SUM is not interchangeable with multiplication; a naive EXP(SUM(LN(x))) fails for zero and negative factors.',contract='Add a product aggregate with explicit NULL/empty, zero, sign, overflow/underflow and numeric-type contracts. The verified common profile skips NULL, returns NULL for an all-NULL frame, preserves negative signs and returns zero when a zero factor is present. A guarded SQL expression works on the tested fixtures; native implementation should be checked against those full outputs.',main='prod',ref='https://docs.dolphindb.com/en/Programming/SQLStatements/analytic_functions.html'),
'extrema':dict(title='extrema positions within sliding frames with deterministic ties',why='Peak/trough age and time-since-anomaly require the location of a frame extreme, not only MIN/MAX value. First and last occurrence differ when the same extreme repeats; this affects recency features and intervention timing.',contract='Support position-of-min/max within the current frame, including earliest and latest ties (imin/iminLast/imax/imaxLast or canonical equivalents). The verified contract is zero-based position within the physical frame; NULL rows occupy positions but cannot win. Standard partition-wide ROW_NUMBER or RANK is not an equivalent sliding-frame position. Value-at-extreme is addressed through the max_by OVER request; ordinary max_by already exists.',main='imaxLast',ref='https://docs.dolphindb.com/en/Programming/SQLStatements/analytic_functions.html'),
'aggregate_over':dict(title='allow max_by and any_value aggregates to compose with OVER',why='Analysts need the payload belonging to a rolling maximum key, a deterministic latest/earliest observation and representative attributes alongside window metrics. MAX(payload) does not select the payload at MAX(key). Ordinary aggregation cannot preserve one output per event.',contract='Extend supported registered aggregates max_by(value,key,tie) and any_value(value) to OVER. max_by(value,-key,-id) expresses the earliest minimum-key payload for this numeric fixture, covering the value-at-extreme use case of atImax/atImin. Preserve explicit tie and NULL contracts; ANY_VALUE may return any allowed frame member. Both functions already execute as ordinary aggregates. #27064 is a completed tumbling-Time-Window arity bug; this request concerns currently rejected ordinary OVER composition.',main='max_by_over',ref='https://docs.matrixorigin.cn/mo/zh/latest/MatrixOne/Develop/read-data/window-function/time-window.html'),
'weighted_quantile':dict(title='native exact weighted percentiles with window support',why='Latency samples with counts, position-size-weighted price distributions and compressed histogram buckets need a weighted distribution. Expanding integer weights into repeated rows is impractical for large counts. Unweighted PERCENTILE_CONT does not compute the same statistic.',contract='Provide an exact weighted-percentile primitive and document its weighted CDF/interpolation convention, supported weights, paired NULL rules and window support. This verified positive-integer-weight profile uses a lower weighted CDF threshold at p=0.5. The weighted P50 is 2 while supported unweighted PERCENTILE_CONT yields 3. Other ClickHouse quantile estimators/interpolation variants are separate contracts, not claimed equivalent. #25144 requests basic ordered-set percentiles, which already execute on this tested build.',main='quantileExactWeighted_entry',ref='https://clickhouse.com/docs/reference/aggregate-functions/reference/quantileexactweighted'),
'ewm':dict(title='exponential-weighted statistics with explicit decay and NULL policies',why='Online anomaly baselines and adaptive volatility need gradual forgetting rather than a hard cutoff. Irregular event-time decay and missing samples require explicit half-life/ignoreNA behavior; a fixed ROWS average is not equivalent.',contract='Provide exponentially weighted mean/variance/std/covariance/correlation with declared alpha/com/span/half-life, adjust, ignoreNA and initialization policies. The verified simple profile is alpha=0.5, adjust=False, no missing samples, first-sample initialization: values 1,2,3,4 produce means 1,1.5,2.25,3.125. A finite historical self-join can emulate that profile, but does not establish a native incremental-state API or performance parity.',main='ewmMean_entry',ref='https://docs.dolphindb.com/en/Functions/e/ewmMean.html'),
 'topn':dict(title='bounded TopN-by-key window statistics with deterministic ties',why='Risk and operational dashboards need averages, sums, covariance or percentiles of the highest-N exposures or most severe observations inside a rolling frame. A partition-wide TOP/LIMIT or ROW_NUMBER is not the same as selecting TopN separately for every moving frame.',contract='Provide frame-local TopN selection and aggregate families (m/tm/cum TopN equivalents), explicit selection key, N, direction and oldest/latest/all tie policy. The verified profile averages the top two eligible values by descending y in the last three physical rows, breaking ties by oldest id; NULL payloads are excluded before selection. A self-join + per-anchor ROW_NUMBER rewrite computes this profile but expands frame members. Define minPeriods and oversized-N behavior; no cross-database performance result is claimed.',main='mavgTopN_entry',ref='https://docs.dolphindb.com/en/Functions/Themes/mTopN.html'),
 'local_rank':dict(title='local sliding-window ranks distinct from partition-wide RANK',why='An alert often asks how unusual the current observation is relative to the most recent observations. Partition-wide rank changes with the entire history and does not answer that local question; assigning a ROWS clause to RANK does not make it frame-local.',contract='Provide moving/time/cumulative local-rank operators or a canonical SQL equivalent with documented competition/dense/average/percentile conventions and direction. The verified business profile is 1-based ascending competition rank of the current non-NULL value in the last three physical rows, returning NULL when the current value is NULL. This is a declared profile, not an assertion about all DolphinDB default rank conventions.',main='mrank_entry',ref='https://docs.dolphindb.com/en/Functions/Themes/mFunctions.html'),
 'valid_windows':dict(title='valid-observation windows and reset-aware cumulative state',why='The last two valid measurements should not shrink to one measurement when a missing row arrives. Session boundaries, regime changes and explicit resets also need cumulative state restarted at a predicate boundary without changing event alignment.',contract='Provide documented valid-observation-count windows and boundary/reset-aware cumulative operators or migration equivalents. The verified last-two-valid SUM carries 8 across a NULL row, whereas two physical rows would not. A separate verified reset-on-flag SUM profile preserves all input rows. SQL joins and staged windows can calculate these profiles; the requested gap is dedicated declarative/API support and explicit state semantics, not inability to compute them. INTERVAL/SLIDING/FILL already work in MO and are not reported missing.',main='movingWindowIndex_entry',ref='https://docs.dolphindb.com/en/Functions/Templates/movingvalid.html'),
 'technical':dict(title='technical-indicator kernels and rolling risk analytics',why='Financial/risk and telemetry workflows commonly use smoothed indicators, drawdown, streaks and local trends. Each library has seeding, warmup and missing-data conventions; repeatedly porting formulas increases maintenance and validation cost.',contract='Add documented SQL/migration equivalents for the missing EMA/DEMA/TEMA/T3/KAMA/Wilder/WMA/SMA/TRIMA family and rolling drawdown/streak/trend operators. Prioritize well-defined profiles with warmup and NULL semantics rather than claiming all TA-Lib defaults are identical. The verified price fixture 100,120,90,110 has cumulative maximum drawdown ratios 0,0,0.25,0.25 using ordinary SQL windows. Bounded-frame drawdown must use frame-local peaks, not the all-history peak. No live DolphinDB/TA-Lib performance comparison is claimed.',main='mmaxDrawdown_entry',ref='https://docs.dolphindb.com/en/Functions/Themes/TALib.html'),
}
def pretty(sql):return sql.replace(' UNION ALL ','\n  UNION ALL ').replace(' FROM (SELECT ','\nFROM (\n  SELECT ')+ ';'
def value(x):return 'NULL' if x is None else format(x,'.10g') if isinstance(x,(float,int)) else str(x)
def result_values(r):
 if r.get('expected') is None:return 'Entry resolution only; no invented engine result'
 if len(r['expected'])==1 and len(r['expected'][0])!=2:return ', '.join(value(x) for x in r['expected'][0])
 return ', '.join(value(row[-1]) for row in r['expected'])
def short_error(r):
 e=r.get('error','');return e[:180].replace('|','/')
def records(key):return [r for r in D['records'] if r['issue_key']==key and r['repeat']==1]
def build(key,spec):
 rows=records(key)
 if key=='pair_stats':rows=[r for r in rows if not r['name'].startswith('beta')]
 if key=='extrema':rows=[r for r in rows if not r['name'].startswith(('atImax','atImin'))]
 primary=next(r for r in rows if r['name']==spec['main'])
 negative=[r for r in rows if not r['control']]
 positive=[r for r in rows if r['control']]
 # Keep one complete executable probe, with an exact table of additional verified cases.
 body=f'''## Why this is needed\n\n{spec['why']}\n\n## Requested behavior and scope\n\n{spec['contract']}\n\n## Tested revision / existing issue check\n\n- MatrixOne commit: `23d8f61252f3017dff218b530817a240206fd762`.\n- Existing issues were searched before filing; related requests/closed fixes are distinguished above.\n- All data below is synthetic. The probe is read-only and requires no persisted tables.\n\n## Executable unsupported case\n\n```sql\n{pretty(primary['sql'])}\n```\n\nActual MO result, reproduced **3/3**:\n\n```text\n{short_error(primary)}\n```\n\n'''
 if primary.get('expected') is not None:body+='Expected values in `id` order for the declared profile: **'+result_values(primary)+'**.\n\n'
 else:body+='This probe checks **entry-name availability only**. A rejection does not establish that the business calculation is impossible or that this is a valid DolphinDB script signature. The requested mathematical operation is defined above and checked by the independent profile below.\n\n'
 body+='## Additional actually executed cases\n\nEach listed rejection was executed three times on the same synthetic fixture.\n\n| Case | MO result | Declared expected values / scope |\n|---|---|---|\n'
 for r in negative:body+='| `'+r['name']+'` | rejected 3/3 | '+result_values(r)+' |\n'
 if positive:
  body+='\n## Working SQL control / alternative\n\nThe following control was executed **3/3** and its **complete output values** were checked, not merely execution success:\n\n```sql\n'+pretty(positive[0]['sql'])+'\n```\n\n'
  body+='| Control | Verified values |\n|---|---|\n'
  for r in positive:body+='| `'+r['name']+'` | '+result_values(r)+' |\n'
  if key=='aggregate_over':body+='\n`ANY_VALUE` has no deterministic winner contract; the observed value was an allowed member of this fixture. The max_by tie control returns oldest/latest payloads 2 and 9 for equal keys. Ordinary aggregation is already supported; OVER composition is the rejected part.\n'
 if key in ['pair_stats','moments','product','extrema','weighted','ignore_nulls','aggregate_over']:
  body+='\nIndependent NumPy/SciPy checks define the declared mathematical profiles. A separate complete ten-row MO SQL rewrite also recomputed all 17 missing DolphinDB SQL primitives and matched every expected value. That verifies those configurations, not every API option or native execution complexity.\n'
 if key in ['groups','exclude','ignore_nulls','distinct','filter']:body+='\nDuckDB executed the equivalent window semantics and supplied the full expected output. The postfix IGNORE NULLS spelling is adapted to DuckDB\'s argument-level spelling without changing the requested semantics.\n'
 for ch in D.get('clickhouse_controls',[]):
  if ch['name'] in [spec['main']] or key=='frame_navigation' and ch['name'] in ['lagInFrame','leadInFrame']:
   body+='\n## Live ClickHouse correctness control\n\nClickHouse 26.9.16.11 executed this explicit SQL and returned the expected result:\n\n```sql\n'+pretty(ch['sql'])+'\n```\n\n```json\n'+json.dumps(ch['rows'],indent=2)+'\n```\n'
   if ch.get('shape_note'):body+='\nReference encoding note: '+ch['shape_note']+'\n'
 if key=='weighted_quantile':
  ch=next(x for x in D['clickhouse_controls'] if x['name']=='quantileExactWeighted')
  body+='\n## Live weighted reference\n\nClickHouse 26.9.16.11 executed the actual parameterized signature:\n\n```sql\n'+pretty(ch['sql'])+'\n```\n\nIt returned weighted P50 **2**, matching an independent weighted-CDF calculation. The MO entry probe uses a flat candidate SQL name only to show absence; it is not ClickHouse\'s curried signature. MO\'s already supported unweighted P50 is **3**, so it is not a substitute.\n'
 body+='\n## Acceptance / regression coverage\n\nRetain the self-contained fixture and assert complete values, NULL bits and row alignment. Add partitions, ties, empty/all-NULL frames, frame-entry/exit and type boundaries appropriate to this operator. Validate named/API availability separately from mathematical rewrites. Benchmark any claimed state/performance advantage with identical data and correctness rules; no speedup is asserted by these small tests.\n\nSubsequent `SELECT 1` succeeded after the test matrix. DolphinDB references are official documentation; no DolphinDB runtime or all-parameter parity is claimed.\n\n## Reference\n\n'+spec['ref']+'\n'
 return dict(key=key,title='[Feature Request]: '+spec['title'],body=body)
def main():
 target=ROOT/'drafts';target.mkdir(exist_ok=True);payloads=[]
 for key,spec in SPECS.items():
  p=build(key,spec);payloads.append(p);(target/(key+'.md')).write_text(p['body'])
 (ROOT/'payloads.json').write_text(json.dumps(payloads,indent=2));print('Ready',len(payloads),'English feature requests; chars',[len(p['body']) for p in payloads])
 # Supplement an existing request rather than duplicate native regression functionality.
 rows=records('regression_existing');main=next(r for r in rows if r['name']=='REGR_SLOPE_entry');control=next(r for r in rows if r['control'])
 comment=f'''Fresh read-only synthetic verification on MatrixOne commit `23d8f61252f3017dff218b530817a240206fd762`, adding concrete business and window acceptance cases to this existing request.\n\n**Why it matters:** rolling local regression is needed for sensor drift, demand trend and asset exposure/beta. Group-level regression alone loses the one-result-per-event rolling feature; pairwise NULL eligibility and zero predictor variance must be defined.\n\n**Actually rejected 3/3:** REGR_SLOPE(y,v) and REGR_INTERCEPT(y,v), both ordinary aggregate and OVER forms.\n\n```sql\n{pretty(main['sql'])}\n```\n\nActual: `{short_error(main)}`.\n\nWindow variant (also actually executed and rejected 3/3):\n\n```sql\n{pretty(next(r for r in rows if r['name']=='REGR_SLOPE_over')['sql'])}\n```\n\n**Working declared-profile control**, executed 3/3 with all output values independently verified:\n\n```sql\n{pretty(control['sql'])}\n```\n\nSlope values in id order: **{result_values(control)}**. The current frame includes the last three physical rows; moments use identical non-NULL (v,y) pairs, and zero variance returns NULL. This small expression control does not establish stability for very large offsets/magnitudes; native regression should use stable centered state.\n\nPlease retain NULL-bit/full-value tests, not only successful execution, and cover bounded ROWS/RANGE, singleton/constant predictors, missing pairs, multiple partitions and output alignment. Official DolphinDB beta and SQL regression interfaces motivate migration, but no live DolphinDB behavior or performance equivalence is claimed. A separate 17-column SQL mathematical rewrite also matched all independent NumPy/SciPy outputs.\n'''
 (ROOT/'regression-comment.md').write_text(comment)
if __name__=='__main__':main()
